    "q-256862": {
      "dom_id": "step-q256862",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)={e}^{x}-{x}^{3}$ in the interval $[3,4].$ Calculate the first two iterations of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point ${x}_{0}=3.6$ Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,{x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-2}}",
      "worked_solution": "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{x}-{x}^{3})^{′}={e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =({e}^{x}-3{x}^{2})^{′}={e}^{x}-6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-3{x}_{n}^{2}}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-6{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=3.6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-3{x}_{0}^{2}}{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-6{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =3.6-\\frac{{e}^{3.6}-3(3.6)^{2}}{{e}^{3.6}-6(3.6)} \\\\  & =3.752\\,135…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-3{x}_{1}^{2}}{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-6{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =(3.752\\,135…)-\\frac{{e}^{3.752\\,135…}-3(3.752\\,135…)^{2}}{{e}^{3.752\\,135…}-6(3.752\\,135…)} \\\\  & =3.733\\,407…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}≈3.752$ and ${x}_{2}≈3.733,$ rounded to three decimal places.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
