    "q-394148": {
      "dom_id": "step-q394148",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Given that the function $f(x)={e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}+5$ has a critical point in the interval $[2,4],$ use the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from ${x}_{0}=3.5$ to estimate the critical point. Round your final answer to two decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}+5)^{′}={e}^{x}-6x, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =({e}^{x}-6x)^{′}={e}^{x}-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-6{x}_{n}}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}({e}^{{x}_{n}}-6)-({e}^{{x}_{n}}-6{x}_{n})}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n}-1)}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=3.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{0}}({x}_{0}-1)}{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3.5}(3.5-1)}{{e}^{3.5}-6} \\\\  & =3.053\\,190…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}-1)}{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3.053\\,190…}(3.053\\,190…-1)}{{e}^{3.053\\,190…}-6} \\\\  & =2.864\\,577…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{2}}({x}_{2}-1)}{{e}^{{x}_{2}}-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2.864\\,577…}(2.864\\,577…-1)}{{e}^{2.864\\,577…}-6} \\\\  & =2.833\\,890…. \\\\ {x}_{4} & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{3}}({x}_{3}-1)}{{e}^{{x}_{3}}-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2.833\\,890…}(2.833\\,890…-1)}{{e}^{2.833\\,890…}-6} \\\\  & =2.833\\,149….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{3}≈2.83$ and ${x}_{4}≈2.83$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is $x≈2.83,$ rounded to two decimal places.",
